          "id": "10",
          "label": "10. Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number",
          "title": "Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number",
          "objective": "To be able to multiply a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number using multiple methods, including the grid method, partitioning numbers To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and tell them they can use a grid to solve this problem. What does this mean? Is it possible to break apart 35 and 28 so those numbers are easier to multiply? What might that look like? How can we organise our thinking to solve the and column method, with renaming. problem? Allow students time to design a grid that might be useful with their partners. If they have never seen this method before, then model its design and provide support by completing some of the grid for them. Allow students to continue to complete the grid and determine how to use those numbers to solve the problem. Once they have understood this method, ask if they know of any other ways to solve the problem and allow them time to try those other methods. Tell the class you are more comfortable with using number bonds. Is that possible when both numbers are 2 digits? What might that look like? Most students will break 35 into 30 and 5, and 28 into 20 and 8. Anticipate this and then follow up with a question about whether or not we can make 30 easier to use (10, 10 and 10). Prompt students to use the column method to show their thinking. Ask them if it is possible to multiply by 30 and then subtract to get the answer. How can we show that? Use the Master section to work through the different methods. During Guided Practice, students are using their knowledge of multiplying by tens to support multiplying by numbers that are greater than 10 but less than 100. --- ---",
          "summary": "This lesson develops students' ability to understand and apply multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number. Key vocabulary: grid/grid method, ones, tens and hundreds. Students explore the concept in small groups using a column method. The teacher then models the method with number bonds, addressing the common misconception that students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values. Students finish with independent guided practice and a workbook task to consolidate the skill."
